  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 259 AM EDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nEarly morning radar shows a batch of welcome rain showers moving \nthrough the eastern half of the UP on the nose of a 850 mb jet as a \nweak shortwave trough skirts the Upper Great Lakes ahead of large \nscale troughing centered upstream over central Canada. Rainfall \namounts have generally been light overnight, with a few spots near \nthe Wisconsin border approaching a quarter inch but most other \nlocations generally limited to a tenth of an inch or less. In terms \nof the large scale pattern, RAP analysis and water vapor imagery \nreveal split flow over North America, with the aforementioned low \nover Canada and a strong surface reflection approaching Hudson Bay \nwhile a cutoff low remains over the Desert Southwest. The bulk of \nthe UP will stay dry between these two features today once the \ncurrent rainfall exits to the east later this morning. This will set \nthe stage for another warm and pleasant fall day as a weak warm air \nadvection regime supports highs in the mid 70s across much of the \narea again today, with morning clouds giving way to a few breaks of \nsunshine by the afternoon.\n\nThursday looks to remain dry over the UP as a low associated with \nthe southern stream energy spins up over the Plains and lifts \nnortheast towards the Great Lakes. The vast majority of guidance \nkeeps any rain associated with this system well to the south over \nWisconsin and Lower Michigan, with low end chances for any \nmeasurable precip generally confined to areas along the Lake \nMichigan shoreline. Temperatures look to run about 8-10 degrees \ncooler across the area on Thursday with the passage a cold front. \nThis will also bring breezy conditions near Lake Superior as winds \nshift west-northwesterly behind the front, bringing gusts of 30+ mph \nfrom the Keweenaw eastward to Whitefish Point. Breezy conditions \npersist into Friday as temperatures continue to moderate into the \nupper 50s to low 60s. Expect some chilly nights beginning Thursday \nnight as strong high pressure builds into the region from the \nnorthern Plains and brings clearing skies and more optimal \nradiational cooling processes. Will likely be dealing with some \nrenewed frost/freeze concerns into the weekend as lows dip near or \neven below freezing in a few interior locations. A weak clipper type \nsystem then brings additional precipitation chances to the area late \nSaturday into Sunday.",
